One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, which can lead to **Path Traversal** attacks. This occurs when an application uses user-supplied input to construct a file path without sufficient validation, allowing an attacker to access files outside the intended directory (like `/etc/passwd`).

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // vulnerable: This code directly concatenates user input with a base directory, 
        // allowing an attacker to use "../" sequences to bypass the sandbox.
        Path rootDir = Paths.get("/var/app/user_data/");
        Path filePath = rootDir.resolve(filename);

        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(filePath);
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        Path rootDir = Paths.get("/var/app/user_data/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();

        // Secure solution: Normalize the path and ensure the resulting path 
        // actually resides within the intended root directory.
        Path filePath = rootDir.resolve(filename).normalize();

        if (!filePath.startsWith(rootDir)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Invalid file path: Access denied.");
        }

        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(filePath);
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
```

